<section>
    <h2>Fill forms the way a user reads them</h2>
    <p>
        A user finds a field by the words next to it, so your test should too. <code>getByLabel</code> finds an
        input by its <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>, and keeps working when someone renames an id or reorders the form.
    </p>
<pre><code>await page.getByLabel('Adventurer name').fill('Merin Ashgrove');</code></pre>
    <p>
        <code>fill</code> clears the field first, then types the whole value and fires the same input events
        a real keyboard would. It's the right call for nearly every text-like input.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>One method per kind of input</h2>
    <table class="lesson-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Input</th><th scope="col">Method</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Text, email, password</td><td><code>fill</code></td><td><code>.fill('Merin')</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Number</td><td><code>fill</code>, as text</td><td><code>.fill('7')</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Date</td><td><code>fill</code>, as <code>yyyy-MM-dd</code></td><td><code>.fill('2026-05-03')</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Dropdown (<code>&lt;select&gt;</code>)</td><td><code>selectOption</code></td><td><code>.selectOption('Cartographers')</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Checkbox</td><td><code>check</code> / <code>uncheck</code></td><td><code>.check()</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Radio button</td><td><code>check</code></td><td><code>getByRole('radio', { name: 'Ranger' }).check()</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        A date input always takes <code>yyyy-MM-dd</code>, whatever format the browser shows on screen. A number
        input still takes a string: <code>fill(String(torches))</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Why <code>check</code> and not <code>click</code>?</strong> A click toggles: on a box
        that's already ticked, it unticks it. <code>check</code> makes sure the box ends up checked, and fails
        if it can't.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Reading values from the page</h2>
    <p>
        Forms are often filled from data that's on the page too: a table, a summary, an email preview. Read it
        with locators, then use it:
    </p>
<pre><code>// A table row's accessible name is its cells' text, so "Guild" finds the Guild row.
const guild = await page.getByRole('row', { name: 'Guild' })
  .getByRole('cell')
  .textContent();

await page.getByLabel('Guild').selectOption(guild!.trim());</code></pre>
    <p>
        Read every value you need first, then fill the form in one go. On a page that's racing a timer, that's
        the difference between a cleared room and a sprung trap.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Check what you entered</h2>
    <p>
        Before you submit, or when a test fails and you're not sure why, assert on the field itself.
        These are web-first assertions, so they wait like the ones on floor 3:
    </p>
<pre><code>await expect(page.getByLabel('Adventurer name')).toHaveValue('Merin Ashgrove');
await expect(page.getByLabel('Guild')).toHaveValue('Cartographers');
await expect(page.getByLabel('I swear this registration is true')).toBeChecked();</code></pre>
    <p>
        In the trap room, a scribe keeps a ledger and won't let you through unless your registration matches it
        exactly. Six seconds. Every kind of input on this scroll, in one form.
    </p>
</section>
